.TH GERANTD 1 2026-11-04 garnish "garnish manual"
.SH NAME
gerantd \- session-bus daemon that runs garnish VMs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gerantd
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B gerantd
starts and stops crosvm virtual machines when asked to over D-Bus. It runs as
your user, without root, and owns
.B org.garnish.Gerant
on the session bus. It is usually started by D-Bus activation, as
.B gerantd.service
in the user's systemd instance, and logs to the journal
.RB ( "journalctl \-\-user \-u gerantd" ).
Run by hand, it logs to standard error.
.PP
Each VM is configured in
.IR ~/.config/garnish/vms/name.conf ;
see
.BR garnish\-vm.conf (5).
The configuration is read on every start, so changes apply the next time a
VM starts.
.SS Starting a VM
.B gerantd
reads and checks the VM's configuration, gets a tap device from
.BR gerant\-net (8)
with
.BR Mode=nat ,
takes the lowest free vsock CID, gets the kernel to boot (without
.BR Kernel= ,
the newest one in the image, from
.BR gerant\-boot (8)),
then starts the helpers the configuration asks for and
.BR "crosvm run" :
.TP
.B passt
the network, with
.B Mode=passt
(the default).
.TP
.B vhost\-device\-sound
sound, with
.BR Audio=yes .
Its playback stream is named
.BI garnish. name
on the host.
.TP
.BI "carte sync \-\-cid " cid
the guest's applications in the host's menu, with
.B AppMenu=yes
or
.BR Wayland=yes ;
see
.BR carte (1).
.TP
.BI "sonnette relay \-\-cid " cid
the guest's notifications, with
.BR Notifications=yes ;
see
.BR sonnette (1).
.TP
.B videur
the clipboard filter, with
.B Wayland=yes
and
.B Clipboard=
other than
.BR both ;
see
.BR videur (1).
.PP
.BR crosvm ,
.BR vhost\-device\-sound ,
.BR passt ,
.BR carte ,
.BR sonnette ,
.B videur
and
.B gerant\-net
are looked up in
.BR PATH .
.B carte
and
.B sonnette
are skipped if they are not installed; a
.B gerant\-net
that is not setuid root is skipped.
.B gerant\-boot
is looked for next to
.B gerantd
first.
.PP
The VM is
.B booting
once crosvm runs, and
.B running
once the guest reports that it has booted: the image's
.B gerant\-ready.service
sends
.B READY=1
to vsock port 8891 on the host. An image without it shows as running after
120 seconds. vsock ports are shared by all users: if another user's
.B gerantd
holds port 8891, VMs show as running as soon as crosvm runs.
.PP
When the guest reboots, crosvm exits with status 32 and
.B gerantd
starts it again with the same resources, booting the newest kernel in the
image again, so a kernel the guest just installed boots. A guest that
reboots more than 5
times in 60 seconds is stopped.
.SS Stopping a VM
.B StopVm
presses the ACPI power button
.RB ( "crosvm powerbtn" ).
If the guest is still running after
.B StopTimeoutSec=
(see
.BR gerantd.conf (5)),
.B gerantd
runs
.BR "crosvm stop" ,
and kills crosvm 5 seconds later if it is still there.
.B KillVm
begins with
.BR "crosvm stop" .
When crosvm exits, the helpers are stopped and the CID and tap are given
back, so a VM's CID and address can change between runs.
.SH D-BUS INTERFACE
Object
.BR /org/garnish/Gerant ,
interface
.BR org.garnish.Gerant1 :
.TP
.BI StartVm( "s name" ") \(-> VmInfo"
Start a VM; returns once crosvm runs.
.TP
.BI StopVm( "s name" )
Press the power button; returns right away.
.TP
.BI KillVm( "s name" )
Run
.BR "crosvm stop" ;
returns right away.
.TP
.B ListVms() \(-> a(VmInfo)
Every configured VM, running or not.
.TP
.BI "signal VmStarted(" VmInfo )
.TQ
.BI "signal VmStopped(" "s name" ", " "s reason" )
.TQ
.BI "signal VmStateChanged(" VmInfo )
.B VmStateChanged
follows a VM through every state, and also reports crosvm's new PID after
a guest reboot.
.PP
.I VmInfo
is
.B (ssusssuss)
.RI ( name ,
.IR state ,
.IR cid ,
.IR tap ,
.IR address ,
.IR user ,
.IR pid ,
.IR log_dir ,
.IR address6 ).
.I state
is
.BR stopped ,
.BR starting ,
.BR booting ,
.B running
or
.BR stopping .
For a stopped VM the other fields are empty or 0.
.PP
Errors are
.BI org.garnish.Gerant1.Error. name
with a message:
.BR NoSuchVm ,
.BR InvalidConfig ,
.BR AlreadyRunning ,
.BR NotRunning ,
.B NoResources
(out of CIDs),
.BR StartFailed ,
.BR ShuttingDown .
.PP
Anything running as your user can call these, just as it could run crosvm.
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.BR SIGTERM ", " SIGINT
Shut every VM down as
.B StopVm
does, then exit. A second signal stops them right away.
.PP
crosvm and the helpers get
.B SIGTERM
if
.B gerantd
dies.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/garnish/vms/name.conf
VM configurations; see
.BR garnish\-vm.conf (5).
.TP
.I ~/.config/garnish/gerantd.conf
What
.B gerantd
hands out to VMs, read at startup; see
.BR gerantd.conf (5).
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/garnish/name/
The VM's logs, emptied on every start:
.I console.log
(the kernel console: the kernel's and systemd's boot messages),
.IR crosvm.log ,
and
.IR passt.log ,
.IR vhost\-device\-sound.log ,
.IR carte.log ,
.I sonnette.log
and
.I videur.log
for the helpers that ran.
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/garnish/name/
The VM's sockets, among them
.I console.sock
for
.BR "gerant console" ,
and the kernel and initrd from its image,
.I vmlinuz
and
.IR initrd .
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/user/gerantd.service
.TQ
.I /usr/share/dbus\-1/services/org.garnish.Gerant.service
The user unit and its D-Bus activation file.
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
$ busctl \-\-user call org.garnish.Gerant /org/garnish/Gerant \e
      org.garnish.Gerant1 StartVm s debian
$ systemctl \-\-user stop gerantd    # shuts every VM down
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR gerant (1),
.BR gerant\-net (8),
.BR gerant\-boot (8),
.BR garnish\-vm.conf (5),
.BR gerantd.conf (5),
.BR crosvm (1),
.BR passt (1),
.BR garnish (7)
